<article class="explainer">
  <header class="explainer-header reading-width">
    <h1>What is the singularity?</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="prose reading-width">
    <p>The technological singularity is a hypothetical turning point at which technological change, often driven by intelligence beyond human capabilities, becomes so rapid or profound that familiar ways of predicting the future break down. It is a family of ideas, rather than a single agreed scientific theory or a date on the calendar.</p>
    <p>One influential version centres on feedback: an AI system helps design a more capable system, which can then accelerate the next round of research. Other versions emphasise human augmentation, using technology to extend people's abilities, or the broader acceleration of technology. Neither the speed nor the inevitability of such a transition is established.</p>

    <h2>Our view</h2>
    <p>This is Singularity Review's editorial opinion, and it is speculative. We believe the technological singularity, understood as the point where AI meaningfully accelerates its own development, has already begun.</p>
    <p>The pace of improvement is one reason. <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2025-03-19-measuring-ai-ability-to-complete-long-tasks/">METR measured</a> that the length of tasks leading AI agents can complete at 50% reliability has doubled roughly every seven months since 2019, and possibly faster since 2024. Agents are AI systems that use tools to carry out tasks. Task length here means the time a human would need, not how long the AI runs. <a href="https://metr.org/notes/2026-01-22-time-horizon-limitations/">METR cautions that individual estimates are imprecise</a>.</p>
    <p>AI is also writing software for AI. <a href="https://fortune.com/2026/01/29/100-percent-of-code-at-anthropic-and-openai-is-now-ai-written-boris-cherny-roon/">Fortune reported on 29 January 2026</a> that Anthropic says 70% to 90% of its code company-wide is AI-generated, and about 90% of Claude Code's own code is written by Claude Code. Outside the labs, reported figures are much lower: Microsoft said about 30% in April 2025.</p>
    <p>There are examples of AI improving the processes used to build AI. In May 2025, <a href="https://deepmind.google/blog/alphaevolve-a-gemini-powered-coding-agent-for-designing-advanced-algorithms/">Google DeepMind reported that AlphaEvolve</a> improved efficiency in Google's data centres, chip design and AI training, including training the language models underlying AlphaEvolve itself. It also found a way to multiply 4x4 complex-valued matrices, grids of complex numbers, using 48 scalar multiplications instead of the 49 in Strassen's 1969 result in that setting. That is a specific mathematical improvement, rather than evidence of progress on every kind of problem.</p>
    <p>AI is taking on research tasks, too. <a href="https://openai.com/index/research-acceleration-view-inside-openai/">OpenAI wrote on 6 September 2026</a> that it had reached its goal of an automated research intern: a system that carries out well-defined research tasks under human direction, including work that would take a skilled researcher a few days. It is aiming for an automated AI researcher by March 2028, but says it does not yet know how to safely get all the way there.</p>
    <p>Mathematical reasoning offers another sign of capability. An advanced version of <a href="https://deepmind.google/blog/advanced-version-of-gemini-with-deep-think-officially-achieves-gold-medal-standard-at-the-international-mathematical-olympiad/">Gemini Deep Think officially achieved gold-medal standard at the 2025 International Mathematical Olympiad</a>. It solved five of six problems, with solutions graded by the competition's coordinators. This shows performance on demanding mathematical tasks; it does not demonstrate independent AI development.</p>
    <p>The speed of adoption gives these changes wider reach. <a href="https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1002265/chatgpt-now-has-1-2-billion-weekly-users-openai-says">According to OpenAI, as reported by The Verge on 29 September 2026</a>, ChatGPT reached 1.2 billion weekly users, up from 400 million in February 2025. Usage alone does not establish a singularity, but it shows how widely these tools are being adopted.</p>
    <p>The strongest counterarguments are that benchmarks are not the real world, humans still direct the work, and much of the industry is far from the figures reported by leading labs. We would change our view if independent evidence showed that these gains do not meaningfully accelerate AI development, or that the apparent feedback loop cannot sustain further progress. For now, we see the beginning of that loop, with its future scale and consequences still uncertain.</p>

    <h2>Where the idea came from</h2>
    <p>In a 1958 recollection of John von Neumann, mathematician Stanislaw Ulam described a conversation about accelerating technology and an approaching singularity in human history. This early use concerned technological change broadly. It was not a detailed prediction about today's AI. <a href="https://www.ams.org/journals/bull/1958-64-03/S0002-9904-1958-10189-5/" rel="noopener">Read Ulam's recollection</a>.</p>
    <p>In 1965, statistician I. J. Good set out a more specific mechanism in <em>Speculations Concerning the First Ultraintelligent Machine</em>. A machine better than humans at intellectual work, he argued, could also design better machines. Those machines could repeat the process, creating what he called an intelligence explosion. Good also raised the problem of keeping such a machine under human control. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2458(08)60418-0" rel="noopener">Read Good's paper</a>.</p>
    <p>Vernor Vinge's 1993 essay, <em>The Coming Technological Singularity</em>, made greater-than-human intelligence central to the idea. He explored several routes to it, including AI, computer networks and closer links between humans and computers, and argued that its consequences would be difficult to foresee. <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19940022856" rel="noopener">Read Vinge's essay</a>.</p>
    <p>Ray Kurzweil brought the idea to a wider audience through books including <em>The Singularity Is Near</em> (2005). His account emphasises accelerating technological progress and the integration of people and machines. Its forecasts remain predictions, not settled findings. <a href="https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/291221/the-singularity-is-near-by-ray-kurzweil/" rel="noopener">Ray Kurzweil's book</a>.</p>

    <h2>Why it matters now</h2>
    <p>AI is already being used to write software and assist research. <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/how-ai-is-transforming-work-at-anthropic" rel="noopener">Anthropic's study of its own engineers</a> offers one account of that shift, with limits that make generalisation difficult. These uses bring the question of AI accelerating AI development into practical focus.</p>
    <p>These examples raise a practical question: how much AI can speed up the development of more capable AI. Their long-term consequences remain uncertain, and there is no agreed test for deciding when a singularity has begun.</p>

    <h2>What remains open</h2>
    <p>Can systems reliably carry out long research projects and improve their own designs? How much will progress depend on human judgement, new data, energy, chips and physical experiments? Could improvements arrive gradually, or reinforce one another quickly?</p>
    <p>There are political questions as well: who controls powerful systems, who benefits from their use and how their behaviour can be evaluated and governed. No agreed test tells us when the singularity begins. The value of the idea lies in examining these possibilities carefully, without mistaking a compelling scenario for an observed fact.</p>
  </div>

